{{- end }}
- Tests never measure time: no real timers or `setTimeout` waits, no bounds
  such as "answers within 500 ms" on `performance.now()` or `Date.now()`.
  They pass in your sandbox and fail on the shared CI runner.
- A budget in the issue or the needs ("within 200 ms", "10,000 items") gets
  a vitest `bench` on realistic input, not a test; put the numbers and the
  input size in the summary. A latency budget in AWS ({{ if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}task start-up, DynamoDB{{ else }}cold start, DynamoDB{{ end }})
  can only be measured in an account: say so.
- Timing logic (timeouts, retries) is tested with fake timers
  (`vi.useFakeTimers()`, then `vi.advanceTimersByTime`), or by awaiting the
  event it fires.
- Say which behaviour needs a person to try in a real AWS account (IAM
  policies at run time, {{ if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}health checks, scaling, deployments{{ else }}throttling, cold starts{{ end }}).
